<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voulet–Chanoine Mission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Voulet–Chanoine Mission was a French military expedition of 1899 led by Captains Paul Voulet and Julien Chanoine, sent from French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> to conquer the territory towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. Its troops massacred villagers and burned settlements across what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, including Birni-N'Konni. When Lieutenant-Colonel Klobb was sent to take command, Voulet had him shot, and both leaders were then killed by their own soldiers in July 1899; the mission went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zinder">Zinder</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voulet-chanoine-mission.jpg" alt="Voulet–Chanoine Mission" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Voulet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The mission, officially the Central African-Chad Mission, was one of three French expeditions meant to meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foureau-lamy-mission">Foureau–Lamy Mission</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and the Gentil Mission from Middle Congo. It had 50 Senegalese Tirailleurs, 20 spahis and 30 interpreters, but its bulk was 400 auxiliaries and 800 porters pressed into service, under nine Europeans. Paul Voulet, who had conceived it to further his career, and Julien Chanoine had already conquered Ouagadougou, the capital of the Mossi Kingdoms. Their orders were vague and sweeping; the British historian Geoffrey Regan judged that they gave carte blanche to two known psychopaths.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At Koulikoro on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> the column split. Chanoine's men pillaged villages for food and shot anyone trying to escape, and dysentery killed 148 bearers in two months. Reunited at Say, the column, now 2,000 strong, looted, raped and killed even in French-controlled areas; at Sansanné-Haoussa on 8 January 1899 it killed one hundred and one people, thirty of them women and children. After Lieutenant Louis Peteau, dismissed on 29 January, described the atrocities in a letter to his fiancée, the government ordered on 20 April that Voulet and Chanoine be arrested. Meanwhile they met resistance from Queen Sarraounia at Lougou.</p><p>In mid-July, after a pursuit of over 2000 km, Lieutenant-Colonel Jean-François Klobb reached Voulet at Dankori, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zinder">Zinder</a>; Voulet had his men shoot him and that evening declared that he would found an empire. On 16 July the Senegalese riflemen killed Chanoine when Voulet fired on them, and a sentry killed Voulet the next morning. Lieutenant Marc Pallier took Zinder on 30 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under Lieutenant Paul Joalland and Lieutenant Octave Meynier the remaining troops joined Foureau, Lamy and Gentil, and the combined force defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kousseri">Battle of Kousséri</a> in April 1900. The publication of the atrocities in August 1899 raised a storm in the press, but the government rejected a parliamentary inquiry in December 1900, and a ministry inquiry closed in 1902 by blaming the heat. The mission was revived in memory by Abdoulaye Mamani's novel Sarraounia of 1980 and Med Hondo's film of 1986.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Voulet–Chanoine Mission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French conquest of the area began with the <b>Voulet-Chanoine mission</b> of 1899, notorious for massacring villagers, and the occupation of Zinder, capital of the Sultanate of Damagaram.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a></li></ul></section>
